  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-2y+z)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-2y+z) & =c \\\\ x-2y+z & ={e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the exponent ${e}^{c}$ is positive for all $-\\infty <c<\\infty .$ If we let ${e}^{c}={r}^{2},$ we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\nx-2y+z={r}^{2},\\,r\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$",
